In computer science , a high  minus  level programming language is a programming language with strong abstraction from the details of the computer . In comparison to low  minus  level programming languages , it may use natural language elements , be easier to use , or may automate ( or even hide entirely ) significant areas of computing systems ( for example memory management ) , making the process of developing a program simpler and more understandable relative to a lower  minus  level language . The amount of abstraction provided defines how `` high  minus  level '' a programming language is . 
